f patty jackson met  bourdain in the kitchen a different one  all restaurants are haunted but this  restaurant for me is very haunted is it  yeah i mean in a nice way they're good  memories but  but there are memories will ever has  gathered these memories for her new book  bourdain the definitive oral biography  the stories from friends and family  paint a picture of a complex man who  never quite settled he was terrified  that he was going to work on the line  for the rest of his life and in the  kitchen yeah and he was scared of that  this is what i've been doing every day  for 28 years that no sane person would  want to do this and he saw writing as a  way out he was hoping that it might be  bourdain's work hard party harder  lifestyle had taken its toll  addicted to heroin he was high when he  first met rose who was running a  literary magazine out of his apartment  bourdain submitted a comic book by mail  i wrote back to this person  your drawing sort of sucks  but your writing is good yeah  and the next thing i know  uh somebody rang my buzzer and there was  this very tall guy there in chefs whites  that guy was bourdain right from the  get-go i knew that he was a talented  writer and a great storyteller rose  helped bourdain get the deal to publish  kitchen confidential changed everything  changed everything for all of us really  it led to bourdain's tv shows no  reservations  and changed everything for nari kai too  meet nari she works in our office she'd  been working for his show no  reservations when she drunkenly  suggested at a wrap party that bourdain  visited her home country korea  he took her up on the offer  nari's enormous energy and excitement  for her homeland is why i've come  growing up in america as an immigrant  there's a lot of racism a lot of  bullying and a lot of shame on my you  know on my part of just wanting to fit  in and it wasn't until tony was like  actually this is your contribution your  unique heritage and your unique point of  view i mean koreans are a brave people i  mean who else would eat this right i  mean  me  and for me as a creative person it  fueled everything that i do well i'm  psyched my dream has finally come true  bourdain's drive for travel and  adventure kept him on the road  constantly let's wrap it up boys we've  got places to go sometimes 250 days a  year what was that all about do you  think i don't think there was enough in  the world sometimes to really keep him  occupied i don't know if he was  trying to escape in some ways from  things or if he was trying to absorb  more things i think that a lot of the  passions that he had  were actually a form of addiction  and the travel  to  and and why he did it even when he was  so run down it wasn't until he passed  away that i realized  that  darkness that he possessed we're always  going going going he did have this the  sadness and i think his work kept him  distracted from that it's a gift  a dream  a curse  the best thing  the happiest thing  yet also  the loneliest thing in the world  bourdain's impact on those around him  was so profound  that when he took his life in 2018 for  many it left a deep void  there's a quote from sally freeman in in  the book and she said when i heard the  news he died i thought so many people  have lost their anchor why was he an  anchor for a lot of people  well i think he was so relatable right  you know sharing his story of of working  hard and having disappointments and and  defeats and also lots of victories  everyone felt connected to him people  who didn't know him felt connected to  him  and still do yeah and still do yeah what  is that power he cared about everybody  astonishing  unbelievable private in new york he was  really loyal  really dedicated person and  i think that translated in his work  he was a seeker he was a dreamer he had  passions and ideas and he found a way to  live that life that's gonna be good this  is something that he left behind is his  passion  to want 